export const bodyHtml = "\n<p>The 96% and the TKL (tenkeyless, also called 80%) are the two layouts people compare when full-size feels too big but they are not ready for a small board. They differ by one block of keys, and that block decides everything.</p>\n<p>Here is how many keys each has, how wide they are, and who should choose which.</p>\n<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> A 96% keyboard keeps the numpad and has about 96 to 99 keys, while a TKL drops the numpad and has about 87. The 96% is wider than a TKL because of that numpad, so pick 96% if you enter numbers often and TKL if you want the most mouse space.</p>\n<h2 id=\"layout-differences\">Layout differences at a glance</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>96% (1800 compact)</th><th>TKL (80%)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Numpad</td><td>Yes, squeezed against the main block</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Function row</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arrow keys</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Navigation block</td><td>Compressed or shared</td><td>Separate block</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Typical key count</td><td>About 96 to 99</td><td>About 87</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Approx. width, calculated key area</td><td>About 15 in</td><td>About 13.7 in</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Sources agree on the key counts but not on exact millimetres, and one notes that the 96% is similar in width to a TKL in some designs. The width figures here are calculated from a 20 unit and an 18.25 unit layout at 0.75 inch per unit, so a real board will be a little larger because of the case.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-the-96-fits-the-numpad\">How a 96% fits a numpad into less space</h2>\n<p>A 96% removes the gaps between the key blocks and shrinks the navigation column to a single column of keys. That is the trick: the numpad stays, but the keys sit shoulder to shoulder. The cost is muscle memory, because arrow keys and the delete cluster sit closer to other keys.</p>\n<h2 id=\"desk-space\">What each uses on a desk</h2>\n<p>Across a 40 inch desk, a 96% leaves about 24 inches for a mouse and everything else, while a TKL leaves a little more than 25. That gap of roughly an inch is smaller than most expect, which is why the numpad decision matters more than the width one.</p><ul><li>On a tight desk with a mouse pad, the extra inch matters less than the numpad itself.</li><li>On a desk with a monitor arm and a laptop, a TKL leaves a cleaner mousing zone.</li><li>With a keyboard tray, check the tray opening against the case width on the listing.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"who-should-pick-the-96\">Who should pick the 96%</h2>\n<p>Choose a 96% if you enter figures, work in spreadsheets, or do data entry and the numpad is part of your day. You get nearly full-size function in a smaller case. See our <a href=\"/guide/best-96-percent-keyboards\">96% keyboard picks</a> for models that keep that layout.</p>\n<h2 id=\"who-should-pick-a-tkl\">Who should pick a TKL</h2>\n<p>A TKL fits gamers, writers and anyone who rarely touches a numpad. It keeps the familiar spacing of the full-size layout, so there is less relearning than with a 96%. The <a href=\"/guide/tkl-vs-full-size-keyboard\">TKL versus full-size guide</a> explains the larger step, and the <a href=\"/guide/best-tkl-ergonomic-keyboards\">TKL ergonomic picks</a> cover comfort-focused models.</p>\n<h2 id=\"mixed-option\">A third option: separate numpad</h2>\n<p>If neither feels right, a TKL plus a small standalone numpad gives you the numpad only when you want it. It costs desk space when attached, but you can slide it away.</p>\n<h2 id=\"faq\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What is a 96% keyboard?</h3>\n<p>A 96% keyboard, also called 1800 compact, keeps almost every key of a full-size board, including the numpad, but packs them tightly. Sources describe it as about 96 to 99 keys.</p>\n<h3>How big is an 80 percent keyboard?</h3>\n<p>An 80% or TKL board is about 14 inches wide by retail descriptions, and around 87 keys. The key area calculates to about 13.7 inches.</p>\n<h3>96 vs 80 keyboard: which is better?</h3>\n<p>Neither is better overall. Choose 96% for numpad access and 80% for more mouse space and standard key spacing.</p>\n<h3>Is a 96% keyboard much smaller than a full-size one?</h3>\n<p>It saves a few inches of width, but it keeps the numpad, so it is a modest reduction rather than a big one.</p>\n<h3>Do 96% keyboards have arrow keys?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Arrows and the function row are kept, although some navigation keys are moved or shared.</p>\n<h3>Is a TKL easier to switch to from full-size?</h3>\n<p>Usually yes. The spacing between key groups is preserved, which makes the transition simpler than a compressed 96% layout.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.rtings.com/keyboard/learn/keyboard-size-guide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RTINGS keyboard size guide</a>: the 96% and TKL key count and numpad trade</li><li><a href=\"https://redragonshop.com/de/blogs/community/mechanical-keyboard-size-guide-60-65-75-tkl\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Redragon layout comparison</a>: TKL width and layout counts from a retail table</li><li><a href=\"https://shop.rapoo.com/blogs/keyboard-guides/1800-keyboard-compact-guide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Rapoo 1800 compact guide</a>: what the 1800 compact style keeps from full-size</li></ul>\n<p>Key counts and widths vary between brands and some sources use different definitions for the 96% layout. Treat the numbers as typical, and check the listing for exact dimensions.</p>";
